=== admin.md 265-300
everything their name is on — the connections they created, the check versions they edited,
and the runs and results underneath the suites they handed over. Erasing a person's data is
a separate, deliberate act on the Compliance page.

**The env-listed caveat.** Membership is the union of the member list and the deployment's
own allowlists, so an address named in an allowlist the deployment actually uses (the OIDC
ones when OIDC is configured, the OTP ones when the OTP mailer is, and `WORKSPACE_ADMIN_EMAILS`
always) still signs in after their row is gone. The pass withdraws the row regardless — both
doors have to close — and then **names the variable you still have to edit**, on the preview
and again on the receipt. An allowlist for a sign-in mode the deployment does not use is
ignored, because it admits nobody. When there is no membership row at all and an allowlist
admits the address, the step is skipped with the same variable named.

The pass also sets the departing user's stored role to **viewer**, audited like any role
change: an offboarded admin must not keep counting toward the last-admin guard, and an
offboarded member must not keep edit rights if an allowlist ever lets them sign in again.
The email you type to confirm is what the server checks — it is never filled in for you.

The closing receipt is the record: what was transferred, how many keys and sessions were
revoked, whether membership was withdrawn, the role change, any environment variable that
still admits the address, and every step that did not run with its reason. Each step also
writes its own audit event, alongside one for the pass as a whole.

### Seed and emergency access

Two deployment settings stay available and are deliberately grant-only, as break-glass:

| Setting | What it does |
|---|---|
| `OIDC_ALLOWED_EMAILS` / `OIDC_ALLOWED_DOMAINS` | Admits addresses on an identity-provider deployment, whether or not the member list names them. |
| `AUTH_OTP_ALLOWED_EMAILS` / `AUTH_OTP_ALLOWED_DOMAINS` | The same for email-code sign-in. Still required at boot in that mode, since the first admin has to come from somewhere. |
| `WORKSPACE_ADMIN_EMAILS` | Grants the Admin role regardless of the stored one (see roles, below). |

If a membership change locks the workspace out, add the address to the matching setting and
restart. That path exists precisely so a mistake in the app is recoverable outside it.

=== admin.md retention
result rows are never deleted by retention — only the samples inside them — so metric
trends survive. Shortening a clock takes effect at the next sweep; lengthening one cannot
bring back what an earlier sweep removed.

## Settings — destructive actions

There is no workspace-level delete: the workspace is the deployment. The last card on the
page points each destructive action at where it lives — deleting or transferring any suite
under [Suites](#suites), deleting a connection from Connections, removing or
[offboarding](#offboarding) a member, and erasing a person's values under
[Compliance](#data-subject-rights). Run history is never purged by hand; the retention
clocks above do that on schedule.

